    notes: "Three is right, and it is the one piece of durable execution that people skip until it bites. The checkpoint goes down after the step runs, so a crash in that window means the step runs again on resume — at-least-once, which is what nearly every durable system actually offers. Harmless for a calculation, expensive for a payment or an email. The fix is in the action, not the checkpoint: make it repeat-safe, usually by handing the downstream system a key it can deduplicate on. Say plainly that our own run_durably has exactly this window."
