    notes: "Four is right, and the mechanism is worth a sentence: wrapping an operation in a step records its inputs AND its outputs, which is what lets a replay skip a finished step instead of running it again. That is a stronger guarantee than our own run_durably, which only saves state after each step. But do not oversell it — people say this 'prevents duplicate execution', and it does only once the record has landed. Crash between doing the thing and recording that you did it and you are back to at-least-once, which is why idempotent steps matter even with a good engine underneath. The category is durable execution and there are a handful of real options — DBOS, Temporal, Inngest, Upstash, Cloudflare Workflows — which differ mostly in how much infrastructure they demand. The database-backed ones are the easy sell because you probably already have a database, and the change to your code is small. Tie it back to the seam: we did not pick one. CheckpointStore is a shape with a SQLite implementation behind it, so choosing a real engine later is an implementation swap rather than a rewrite. That is the whole argument for writing the interface down first."
